# ledger.jsonl is append-only and is meant to merge line-for-line with whatever
# arc-gateway produces later. A CRLF translation on checkout would change every
# line's bytes and break that merge, and any hash taken over the ledger with it.
# Same reasoning as engine-rig/.gitattributes.
* text eol=lf

# A14 (2026-07-29). The four campaign artefacts were written by the harness on
# Windows in Python text mode, so their bytes on disk are CRLF -- and the sha256
# that battery/runs/20260728T061147Z-v3/MANIFEST.json pinned when it consumed
# them as evidence was taken over those CRLF bytes. Under the `* text eol=lf`
# rule above, `git add` would normalise them to LF and every clone would then
# hold a file whose digest no longer matches the one already cited. That is a
# silent break in the evidence chain, so eol translation is switched off for
# these paths and the bytes are stored verbatim. `diff` keeps them readable in
# review despite -text. Do not "fix" the line endings: the digests are the point.
out/campaign/*.json -text diff
